A global-scope generated label needs its own counter.

`declare_function` resets `current_count` to 0 on every function. That is
correct for every user of the counter but one: they all build

    create_unique_id("", function->s, number_string)

so the FUNCTION NAME disambiguates and restarting the numbers per function is
what keeps them short. A global string initialiser has no function to name --
it uses the literal "g" --

    create_unique_id("STR_", "g", number_string)          cc_core.c

-- so the numbers restart underneath it and two globals separated by any
function declaration both get STR_g_0.

hex2's `AddHash` inserts at the HEAD of the bucket and `GetTarget` returns the
first match, so the LAST definition wins and every earlier global with the same
number silently reads the later one's bytes. Nothing warns: M1 and hex2 both
accept a duplicate label without comment.

MEASURED, in libtcc.c compiled by micro-c -- 41 colliding labels, e.g.

    :GLOBAL_STR_g_0_contents
    "alm.?"
    ...
    :GLOBAL_STR_g_0_contents
    "%s/lib%s.so"

Two different strings, one name. Every reference to the first resolves to the
second.

THIS WAS IN THE BINARY WE WERE ALREADY SHIPPING. It is not a regression from
the narrowing-cast round; the working mc-tcc has the identical collisions. It
was found by grepping the joined .M1 for duplicate labels while chasing an
unrelated SIGBUS, not by any test -- because no test could see it. A collision
needs two global string initialisers with a function declaration between them,
and the 87-case suite and the twelve end-to-end programs are each a single
small unit. A whole class of fault sat under a green suite.

THE FIX is one more counter, `current_count_global`, that nothing resets.
Deliberately NOT "stop resetting current_count": the reset is load-bearing for
the eight other call sites, and removing it would grow every function-local
label for no benefit.

WHAT IS LEFT, AND WHY IT IS BENIGN. 39 duplicate :GLOBAL_ labels remain in the
joined unit -- errno, file, anon_sym, char_pointer_type, reg_classes and so on.
Those are TENTATIVE DEFINITIONS: tcc.h declares them with ST_DATA (empty under
ONE_SOURCE) and the .c file defines them again. Every one emits `NULL`, so all
references bind to one definition whose contents are identical to the other's.
Consistent, and it costs only space. Checked rather than assumed -- the STR_g
collisions were harmful precisely because their contents DIFFERED, and that is
the test that separates the two cases.

Case 103. Against the compiler without this patch it returns 59 -- alpha and
beta read the wrong strings and all three addresses collide; with it, 0.
difftest aarch64 87/0 -> 88/0; the twelve end-to-end programs stay 12/12.
--- a/cc_core.c
+++ b/cc_core.c
@@ -44,6 +44,24 @@
 char* continue_target_num;
 struct token_list* break_frame;
 int current_count;
+/* A SEPARATE COUNTER FOR GLOBAL-SCOPE GENERATED LABELS.
+ *
+ * current_count is reset by declare_function, which is correct for every other
+ * user of it: they all build `create_unique_id("", function->s, n)`, so the
+ * FUNCTION NAME disambiguates and restarting the numbers per function is what
+ * keeps them short. A global string initialiser has no function to name -- it
+ * uses the literal "g" -- so sharing that counter meant two globals separated
+ * by any function declaration both got STR_g_0.
+ *
+ * hex2's AddHash inserts at the head of the bucket and GetTarget returns the
+ * first match, so the LAST definition wins and the FIRST global silently reads
+ * the other's bytes. In libtcc.c alone that produced 41 colliding STR_g labels
+ * -- e.g. STR_g_0_contents defined as both "alm.?" and "%s/lib%s.so".
+ *
+ * Invisible to every test here: the case suite and the twelve end-to-end
+ * programs are each a single small unit, and a collision needs two global
+ * string initialisers with a function declaration between them. */
+int current_count_global;
 int Address_of;
 
 /* A PREFIX ++/-- WHOSE TARGET IS REACHED THROUGH `->`.
@@ -5972,8 +5990,8 @@
 			 *
 			 * A generated id is both valid and unique. Found by ASSEMBLING the
 			 * output: it parsed and emitted happily for the whole run. */
-			char* number_string = int2str(current_count, 10, TRUE);
-			current_count = current_count + 1;
+			char* number_string = int2str(current_count_global, 10, TRUE);
+			current_count_global = current_count_global + 1;
 			char* name = create_unique_id("STR_", "g", number_string);
 			globals_list = emit("&GLOBAL_", globals_list);
 			globals_list = emit(name, globals_list);
